      "id": "c88fee71d95064eb91dce5055e060f78299fe60b",
      "message": "OTA-1818 \u2014 the touch reaches the region\n\nTelemetry only. Closes the blind interval between T0 (root touch observed)\nand T1 (a handler ran) that the Apple-freeze forensics could not see through.\nBefore this, a terminal root-only run told us a finger landed and nothing\nanswered \u2014 but not whether the touch reached the content that owns the\ncontrols, or stopped short of it.\n\nJob A \u2014 touchPath.ts gains a 'content' stage and two read-only helpers.\n  peekPendingTouchId() reads the newest unclaimed, unexpired pending touch\n  without claiming it, so a region marker never competes with a real handler\n  for the claim. noteContentTouch(region) stamps 'content' against that id\n  and returns it, or returns null when there is no live touch to attach to.\n\nJob B \u2014 ExplorationScreen gains two capture-phase region observers: 'feed'\n  and 'controls'. Two regions, not one: a single controls marker would have\n  read a benign feed tap as interception. Both observers return false, so\n  neither claims the responder and neither changes who handles the touch.\n\nJob C \u2014 InventoryScreen gains the same root observer the other screens have,\n  a rendered touch-path context, and a dispatch/done trace around the Equip\n  slot chain. Equip's T1 and M0 are NOT instrumented: BrandedModal's button\n  descriptor exposes only onPress, and reaching them would require editing a\n  fourth production file outside this job's authorized scope. The omission is\n  named in the source and asserted in the suite so nobody re-derives it.\n\nThe OTA-1813 guard is narrowed, not weakened: its capture-handler window was\nmatching the whole JSX subtree beneath an opener (one measured window ran to\n29,714 characters and swallowed an unrelated predicate), so it now matches\nonly the arrow body, and asserts the handler count equals the opener count so\na handler that stops matching the flat shape fails loudly instead of being\nskipped. Re-verified against the negative control: still caught, by both suites.\n\nNATIVE: no. GAMEPLAY: no. LAYOUT: no. RESPONDER CLAIM: no (3x return false,\n0x return true). PERSISTENCE: no. NEW TIMER: no. NEW STORAGE KEY: no.\n\nFREEZE REPAIR CLAIMED: NO. Apple-freeze causation remains UNKNOWN. This ships\nthe instrument, not the cure; the next step is to wait for natural device\nevidence, not to propose a fix.\n\nValidation: OTA-1818 suite 23/23; regression set 9 suites / 213 tests;\n22/22 source gates by exit code; typecheck:ci clean; typecheck:tests at\nbaseline 201; lint clean; fast surface 1363/1363 suites, 14,497 tests.\nThree negative controls each proved their safeguard and were restored\nmd5-verified with zero residue.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LGFtzR6AQJbu4XdR28y3fX",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-14T11:04:51Z",
